Fixed. The bug was in `Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:5-7`.

**Root cause:** the old code split the text on spaces and kept tokens starting with `#`. That only works when a tag is space-delimited. A tag glued to punctuation (`,#urgent`) never begins with `#`, and a tag wrapped in punctuation (`(#ideas)`) retains the surrounding characters — so neither was extracted.

**Fix:** replaced the space-split with an `NSRegularExpression` match for `#` followed by one or more tag characters (`\p{L}` letters, `\p{N}` numbers, plus `._-`), stripping the leading `#`. Matches are returned in document order, so ordering is preserved.

Verified against the reported cases plus regressions:
- `shopping list,#urgent` → `["urgent"]` ✓
- `(#ideas)` → `["ideas"]` ✓
- existing `hello #world #swift` → `["world","swift"]` ✓ (unchanged)
- `just plain text` → `[]` ✓ (unchanged)
- ordering across mixed punctuation preserved ✓

Note: `swift test` couldn't run in this environment (`sandbox-exec: Operation not permitted`), so I compiled the exact source logic with `swiftc` and ran the cases directly — all pass.


